The centripetal force needed to keep the stone moving at $5 \mathrm{~m} \mathrm{~s}^{-1}$ is

F_c=\frac{m v^2}{r}=\frac{(1 \mathrm{~kg})\left(5 \mathrm{~ms}^{-1}\right)^2}{0.5 \mathrm{~m}}=50 \mathrm{~N}

The weight of the stone is $W=m g=(1 \mathrm{~kg})\left(9.8 \mathrm{~m} \mathrm{~s}^{-2}\right)=9.8 \mathrm{~N}$.
At the top of the circle,

T=F_c-W=50 \mathrm{~N}-9.8 \mathrm{~N}=40.2 \mathrm{~N}

Here, $\omega=2 \hat{k}$
\vec{r}=2 \hat{i}+2 \hat{j}

Linear velocity, $\vec{v}=\vec{\omega} \times \vec{r}$
\begin{aligned}
& =2 \hat{k} \times(2 \hat{i}+2 \hat{j}) \\
& =-4 \hat{i}+4 \hat{j}
\end{aligned}

The kinetic energy of the projectiles is maximum at the point of release. The ratio of K.E. of projectiles

\begin{aligned}
& \frac{\frac{1}{2} m u_1^2}{\frac{1}{2} m u_2^2}=\frac{4}{1} \\
& \frac{u_1^2}{u_2^2}=\frac{4}{1}
\end{aligned}

The ratio of maximum heights of projectiles

\frac{H_1}{H_2}=\frac{4}{1}

\begin{aligned}
& \frac{u_1^2 \sin ^2 \theta_1}{2 g} \\
& \frac{u_2^2 \sin ^2 \theta_2}{2 g}=\frac{4}{1} \\
& \frac{u_1^2}{u_2^2} \cdot \frac{\sin ^2 \theta_1}{\sin ^2 \theta_2}=\frac{4}{1} \\
& \frac{4}{1} \cdot \frac{\sin ^2 \theta_1}{\sin ^2 \theta_2}=\frac{4}{1}
\end{aligned}

(from equation (i))

\begin{aligned}
& \sin ^2 \theta_1=\sin ^2 \theta_2 \\
& \theta_1=\theta_2
\end{aligned}

Now, ratio of ranges of projectiles

\begin{aligned}
& \frac{R_1}{R_2}=\frac{u_1^2 \sin 2 \theta_1}{u_2^2 \sin 2 \theta_2} \\
& \frac{R_1}{R_2}=\frac{u_1^2 \sin 2 \theta_1}{u_2^2 \sin 2 \theta_1} \quad\left(\because \theta_1=\theta_2\right) \\
& \frac{R_1}{R_2}=\frac{u_1^2}{u_2^2}, \frac{R_1}{R_2}=\frac{4}{1}
\end{aligned}
